---
.title = "Exe",
.description = "Bind device buffers, run a compiled executable, retrieve its outputs, and handle ownership.",
.date = .date("2026-11-17T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
.custom = .{ .doc_section = "API Reference" },
---
[`zml.Exe`](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/exe.zig)
is the loaded executable returned by [Platform compilation]($link.page('zml/api-reference/platform').ref('compile-a-model-or-function')).
Pass buffers in the structure and order of the compiled arguments, using the
expected sizes, types, and shards.
The [linear layer example]($link.page('zml/developer-guide/write-your-first-model').ref('compile-and-call-the-layer'))
shows how to bind buffers and read back the output. The call forms below
use a `std.mem.Allocator` for `allocator` and a `std.Io` for `io`.
A return type with `!` can carry an error.

# [Bind arguments and allocate results]($heading.id('exe'))

- `exe.args(allocator)`\
  `!Exe.Arguments`. Allocate argument slots for the executable.
- `arguments.set(buffers)`\
  `void`. Bind the provided buffers to the argument slots.
- `exe.results(allocator)`\
  `!Exe.Results`. Allocate result slots for the executable.

# [Run the executable and read results]($heading.id('run-and-read-results'))

- `exe.call(io, arguments, &results, .{ .wait = true })`\
  `void`. Run with the bound buffers and fill the result slots. `wait` defaults to `false` when omitted.
- `results.get(zml.Buffer)`\
  `Buffer`. Retrieve the output of a single-output call. For multiple outputs, pass the matching structure of buffers.
- `exe.eval(allocator, io, buffers)`\
  `!Buffer`. Run a single-output executable, wait, and return an owned result buffer.

# [Release slots and buffers]($heading.id('ownership-and-cleanup'))

- `exe.deinit()`\
  `void`. Release the loaded executable.

Release argument and result slots with `deinit(allocator)`. Retrieve each
output once per execution and release each owned buffer with `buffer.deinit()`.
When the compiled computation donates an input, execution invalidates its
previous contents. A raw `call` leaves cleanup of its input handles to the
caller. `eval` consumes donated handles.
